## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, cache-line considerations, and safe reuse of barriers in multi-threaded code.

## Key Concepts

- Sense-reversing barrier pattern
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Atomic operations and memory_order semantics

## Code Example

```c
// barrier_init
void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) { b->n = n; b->count = 0; b->sense = false; }

// barrier_wait
void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, bool *local) {
    bool local_sense = !*local;
    if (atomic_fetch_add(&b->count, 1) == b->n - 1) {
        atomic_store(&b->count, 0);
        atomic_store(&b->sense, local_sense);
    } else {
        while (atomic_load(&b->sense) != local_sense) pause();
    }
    *local = local_sense;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to dynamic N?
- How to measure wake-up latency under contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a low-level, lock-free queue system with NUMA locality, ABA avoidance, and fair work-stealing, plus concrete microbenchmarking plans.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffers with sequence counters for ABA avoidance
- NUMA-aware locality and cross-node stealing
- Per-thread local queues and a global steal pool
- Cache-line alignment and memory fencing
- Microbenchmark design for concurrency and contention

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: ring enqueue/dequeue with sequence counters
class Slot { constructor(){ this.seq=0; this.data=null } }
class Ring { constructor(n){ this.size=n; this.mask=n-1; this.buf=Array(n).fill(null).map(()=>new Slot()); this.head=0; this.tail=0 }
  enqueue(x){ const pos = this.head & this.mask; const s = this.buf[pos]; if (s.seq !== this.head) return false; s.data = x; s.seq = this.head + 1; this.head++; return true }
  dequeue(){ const pos = this.tail & this.mask; const s = this.buf[pos]; if (s.seq !== this.tail+1) return null; const v = s.data; s.data=null; s.seq = this.tail + 2; this.tail++; return v }
}}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to more NUMA nodes and maintain fairness? 
- What backoff strategy and memory ordering would you choose under high contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this design for power-failure resume without data loss? 
- How to choose N and payload size for latency/throughput trade-offs in a busy IPC path?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory hierarchy and data locality by forcing cache-friendly tiling in a simple kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Tile size vs cache capacity; 2D blocking; stride-1 vs stride-N access patterns; bounds handling.
- Prefetching to hide memory latency; ensure no aliasing via separate buffers.

## Code Example
```c
void transpose_tiled(const float* A, float* B, int N) {
  const int Bsz = 32;
  for (int br = 0; br < N; br += Bsz) {
    for (int bc = 0; bc < N; bc += Bsz) {
      for (int i = 0; i < Bsz; ++i) {
        int r = br + i;
        if (r >= N) break;
        for (int j = 0; j < Bsz; ++j) {
          int c = bc + j;
          if (c >= N) break;
          B[c * N + r] = A[r * N + c];
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle N not divisible by 32; impact on correctness and performance?
- How would you adapt to double precision or non-square matrices?
- Would you vectorize with AVX/NEON and what changes? How to measure speedup?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design a low-latency, cache-friendly memory allocator with cross-thread reclamation. It evaluates understanding of ABA avoidance, per-size caches, and NUMA implications on modern multi-socket systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Lock-free, per-size-class slab allocator** with thread-local caches to minimize synchronization overhead
- **Cross-thread transfer** via a global lock-free pool enabling efficient memory sharing between threads
- **ABA avoidance** using pointer tagging (e.g., utilizing low bits as a collision counter)
- **Epoch-based reclamation** versus hazard pointers for safe memory reuse
- **Cache-line alignment** to prevent false sharing and control fragmentation

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    void *next;           // Free list pointer
    uintptr_t tag;        // ABA prevention counter
} slab_object_t;

typedef struct {
    void *slabs;          // Pointer to slab pages
    _Atomic(slab_object_t *) free_list[4];  // Per-size free lists
    _Atomic(uintptr_t) tag;                // Global ABA tag
} thread_cache_t;

typedef struct {
    void *slab;
    size_t size_class;
    _Atomic(struct global_pool *) next;
} global_pool_t;

// Thread-local cache declaration
thread_local thread_cache_t tls_cache;

// Allocation API
void *slab_alloc(size_t size) {
    int idx = get_size_class_index(size);
    slab_object_t *obj = atomic_load(&tls_cache.free_list[idx], memory_order_acquire);

    while (obj) {
        slab_object_t *next = obj->next;
        uintptr_t expected_tag = obj->tag;

        if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&tls_cache.free_list[idx], &next,
            (slab_object_t *)((uintptr_t)obj->next | ((expected_tag + 1) << 2)),
            memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed)) {
            return obj;
        }
    }

    // Cache miss: fetch from global pool
    return fetch_from_global_pool(idx);
}

// Free API
void slab_free(void *ptr, size_t size) {
    int idx = get_size_class_index(size);
    slab_object_t *obj = (slab_object_t *)ptr;
    obj->tag = atomic_fetch_add(&tls_cache.tag, 1) + 1;

    do {
        obj->next = tls_cache.free_list[idx];
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&tls_cache.free_list[idx], &obj->next,
        obj, memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
}

// Epoch-based reclamation (pseudocode)
void epoch_reclaim(slab_object_t *obj) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&current_epoch, 1);
    if (atomic_load(&retired_count) > THRESHOLD) {
        // Safely free objects from previous epoch
        sweep_retired_objects();
    }
}
```

## Performance Considerations

### Fragmentation
- Internal fragmentation is bounded by the per-size-class design (worst case: ~50% for objects slightly larger than half the slab size)
- External fragmentation remains minimal since slabs are fixed-size and预先 allocated

### Cache Alignment
- Align each object to cache line size (64 bytes) or use padding to eliminate false sharing between threads
- Global pool structures should occupy separate cache lines from thread-local data

### NUMA Awareness
- On a 2-socket x86-64 system, each socket should maintain a local slab pool
- Affinitize threads to sockets using `sched_setaffinity()` or platform-specific NUMA APIs
- Prefetch memory from the local NUMA node to minimize cross-socket memory access latency

## Proposed Microbenchmark

1. **Workload Configuration**: Run 4 producer-consumer thread pairs, each producing and consuming objects of varying sizes (32/64/128/256 bytes) at configurable rates

2. **Metrics to Measure**:
   - Allocation latency (p50, p99, p999 percentiles)
   - Free latency
   - Cache miss rate via performance counters
   - Cross-NUMA memory transfers

3. **Test Patterns**:
   - Uniform random allocation/free
   - Bursty workload (bulk allocations followed by bulk frees)
   - Strided access patterns to stress NUMA skew

